{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And the voice of the shofar.</b> It is the way of things that the voice of the shofar at the beginning is stronger, but this was the opposite. Perhaps HaShem did this so that it would not be strong at the beginning, lest their hearts depart from them in fear:" "<b>Moses shall speak.</b> According to the Gaon [Saadia], whenever HaShem would pass along the voice of the shofar, Moses would speak in a loud voice, and HaShem would answer him in a voice, in the manner of \"and he heard the voice speaking to him\" (Numbers 7:89). And upon this it is written, \"so that the people may hear when I speak with you.\" And I have already told you that in my opinion, \"when I speak with you\" refers to the Ten Commandments that He would speak:" "And the meaning of \"Moses shall speak\" is cleaving to the voice of the shofar, for the soul of Moses was cleaving to HaShem. For Moses was speaking at the bottom of the mountain with HaShem to ask of Him, and the verse did not disclose what he was asking. And HaShem would answer him in a voice that Moses would hear, and the voice of the shofar, even though it was strong, did not prevent him from hearing the voice. And because of this it is written after it, \"And YHWH called to Moses to go up to the top of the mountain\" (Exodus 19:20), where the glory of YHWH had descended."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "Verse reference (Exodus 19:20) inferred from the phrase quoted; Ibn Ezra writes only \"and it is written after it\" without a citation."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:59.873654934Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}